{"author":{"id":"2eecc7d99b502c158aacb0868dca9358610edc02724d21fe49b0196402d3364e"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1784881567,"nonce":"QnYUO3ryoRR0PaziFX093vLLETA=","title":"ADR-0060 — Org settings surface (`docs/adr/0060-org-settings-connections-and-secret-management.md`).","message":"The global **Settings** area and its first section: **Org Secrets**. Introduce a new top-level `/settings` route in the web-ui, gated on `Administer` at the Org scope and **hidden from non-admins** (the nav entry does not render for them). Under it, an **Org Secrets** section does full CRUD against the *org* scope of the existing `/v1/secrets` API (org-only query → `SecretScope::Org`) — which the backend already supports but no UI reaches today.\n\nThere is exactly one implicit Org (single-tenant experience) — no org switcher, no org picker. \"Org settings\" is the settings of that one org.\n\n\n- [ ] A `/settings` route exists; its nav entry renders only for principals with `Administer` on the Org, and direct navigation by a non-admin is rejected/blank.\n- [ ] Org Secrets section lists org-scoped secret names (never values), and supports add + delete at org scope.\n- [ ] Writes require `Administer`; a non-admin request is refused server-side (not just UI-hidden).\n- [ ] An org-scoped secret set here participates in the `env → repo → org` resolution chain (verifiable: a pipeline referencing the key with no repo/env override resolves to the org value).\n- [ ] Functional test: real router + fakes, org-scope put/list/delete + Administer enforcement.\n\n\nNone - can start immediately.","files":null}]}